<%#
  The one way a production's or a date's ticketing says "my own, not the one
  above" (Tim, 2026-11-13: "we only have one way for toggling an override...
  I still feel like we have multiple ways of doing that"). The head of a
  titled white box: the title, then the override on a line of its own (Tim,
  the same day: "bolted onto the top right... it should be its own line"):
  the switch first, then "Override your box office's comps for Sophomoric
  Songs", the overridden settings a link to where they're set. Put it first inside
  a box that carries data-controller="fieldset-toggle", with the settings in
  a <fieldset data-fieldset-toggle-target="fields"> that's disabled while off.

  It replaced: a pink strip with "own rules", a bordered row with "for this
  date only", a switch in a box's corner, lists that began "Use your
  default", boxes that said "Blank means the production's", and an ages
  choice whose first card followed the production.

  Locals:
    title      the box's heading
    what       what's overridden, in the line's words (default: the title,
               lower case, and "settings": "comps settings")
    follows    whose settings they are: "your box office" or "Laugh-Along, Live!"
    settings_path  where those settings are set (the line links there)
    only       who the override is for: the production's name, or "this date"
    name, id   the switch's param name and DOM id
    checked    on (its own) or off
    intro      an optional sentence under the title
    data       extra data for the switch (auto-submit), merged with the toggle's
%>
<%
  switch_data = { fieldset_toggle_target: "switch", action: [ "change->fieldset-toggle#toggle", local_assigns.dig(:data, :action) ].compact.join(" ") }
  switch_data = (local_assigns[:data] || {}).merge(switch_data)
  what = local_assigns[:what].presence || "#{title.to_s.downcase_first} settings"
  whose = follows == "your box office" ? "your box office's" : possessive(follows)
  overridden = "#{whose} #{what}"
  label = "Override #{overridden} for #{only}"
%>
<div data-own-switch="<%= id %>">
  <div class="px-6 py-4 border-b border-gray-200">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900"><%= title %></h2>
    <% if local_assigns[:intro].present? %><p class="text-sm text-gray-600 mt-1"><%= intro %></p><% end %>
  </div>
  <%# The switch, then the words; the settings being overridden are a link
      to where they're set. Nothing under the line: the box says the rest. %>
  <div class="px-6 py-3.5 border-b border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 flex items-center gap-3" data-override-line>
    <%= render "shared/toggle_switch", name: name, checked: checked, hidden_false: true, id: id, aria_label: label, data: switch_data %>
    <span class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-900">
      <label for="<%= id %>" class="cursor-pointer">Override</label>
      <% if local_assigns[:settings_path].present? %><%= link_to overridden, settings_path, class: "text-pink-600 hover:text-pink-700 underline" %><% else %><%= overridden %><% end %>
      <label for="<%= id %>" class="cursor-pointer">for <%= only %></label>
    </span>
  </div>
</div>
